for f,t,s,c in [("article-term.svg","48 vs 84 months","Which loan term costs less?","#1f4e79"),("article-used.svg","New vs used","Which car loan is cheaper?","#173b5c"),("article-down.svg","Down payment","How much should you put down?","#24607a"),("article-early.svg","Pay it off early","Save interest on your car loan","#2c4a6e")]:
    open("images/"+f,"w").write(svg(t,s,c))
arts.extend([
("car-loan-term-48-60-72-84-months","Car Loan Term Compared: 48, 60, 72 or 84 Months?","Compare monthly payment and total interest for 48, 60, 72 and 84 month car loans, with a worked example and advice on choosing a term.","article-term.svg","Chart comparing 48, 60, 72 and 84 month car loan terms",
'''<p>The loan term is the lever that changes your payment the most. A longer term lowers the monthly bill, but you pay for it in interest. Here is a clear comparison so you can choose with real numbers.</p>
<h2>Same loan, four terms</h2><p>Example: you borrow $26,000 at 6.5% APR.</p>
<ul><li><strong>48 months:</strong> about $616 a month, about $3,590 total interest.</li><li><strong>60 months:</strong> about $509 a month, about $4,510 interest.</li><li><strong>72 months:</strong> about $437 a month, about $5,460 interest.</li><li><strong>84 months:</strong> about $386 a month, about $6,430 interest.</li></ul>
<p>Going from 48 to 84 months cuts the payment by roughly $230 but adds about $2,840 in interest.</p>
<h2>The hidden risk of long loans</h2><p>Cars lose value quickly. On a long loan your balance falls slowly, so you can owe more than the car is worth. This is called negative equity, and it hurts if the car is totaled or you want to sell. Lenders also often charge higher rates on longer terms.</p>
<h2>How to choose</h2><ul><li>Pick the shortest term whose payment fits your budget comfortably.</li><li>If you need a long term to afford the car, consider a cheaper car instead.</li><li>Choose a loan with no prepayment penalty, so you can pay extra when you can.</li></ul>
<p>Test your own price and rate in the calculator by switching the term menu and comparing the total interest.</p>'''),
("new-vs-used-car-loan","New vs Used Car Loan: Rates, Payments and Which Is Cheaper","Understand how new and used car loans differ in interest rate, depreciation and monthly cost, and how to decide which is better for you.","article-used.svg","Comparison of a new and a used car loan",
'''<p>New cars often come with lower advertised rates. Used cars cost less to buy. Which loan is cheaper depends on the whole picture, not only the rate.</p>
<h2>Interest rates</h2><p>Lenders usually charge less for new cars because the car is worth more as security. Used-car loans often carry a higher APR, and the gap can be a few percentage points. Rates change often, so compare live offers from a bank, a credit union and the dealer.</p>
<h2>Depreciation</h2><p>A new car typically loses a large share of its value in the first years. A used car has already taken much of that hit. A lower price means you borrow less, so even at a higher rate the interest in dollars can be smaller.</p>
<h2>What to compare</h2><ul><li><strong>Total cost:</strong> price, tax, fees and interest together.</li><li><strong>Insurance:</strong> newer cars often cost more to insure.</li><li><strong>Repairs:</strong> a new car has a warranty. A used car may need repairs sooner.</li></ul>
<h2>Quick example</h2><p>A $35,000 new car at 5.5% for 60 months costs about $669 a month. A $24,000 used car at 8% for 60 months costs about $487 a month. The used car has the higher rate, yet the lower payment and the lower total cost.</p>
<p>Enter both cars into the calculator and compare the total interest and total cost lines.</p>'''),
("how-much-down-payment-on-a-car","How Much Down Payment Should You Put on a Car?","Learn how much to put down on a new or used car, how a down payment cuts your monthly payment and interest, and the 20% guideline.","article-down.svg","Illustration showing how a down payment reduces a car loan",
'''<p>A bigger down payment is the simplest way to lower your car loan cost. It reduces what you borrow, and every dollar you do not borrow is a dollar that never earns interest for the lender.</p>
<h2>The common guideline</h2><p>Many buyers aim for about 20% down on a new car and about 10% on a used one. New cars lose value fastest, so a larger down payment helps you avoid owing more than the car is worth.</p>
<h2>What the numbers look like</h2><p>At 6.5% APR for 60 months, borrowing $30,000 costs about $587 a month and about $5,210 in interest. Borrowing $24,000 costs about $470 a month and about $4,170 in interest. Putting $6,000 more down saves about $117 a month and about $1,040 in interest.</p>
<h2>Do not drain your savings</h2><p>Keep an emergency fund of at least a few months of expenses. A smaller down payment and a safe cushion is better than a large down payment and no savings.</p>
<h2>Ways to raise your down payment</h2><ul><li>Use your trade-in value.</li><li>Wait and save a few more months.</li><li>Choose a slightly cheaper car.</li><li>Check for manufacturer rebates that count as a down payment.</li></ul>
<p>Try different down payment amounts in the calculator to see your payment and interest change instantly.</p>'''),
("how-to-pay-off-a-car-loan-early","How to Pay Off a Car Loan Early and Save Interest","Find out how extra payments shorten your car loan and cut interest, what to check before paying early, and the best ways to do it.","article-early.svg","Illustration of paying off a car loan early",
'''<p>Paying a car loan off early can save real money. Because interest is charged on your remaining balance, every extra dollar that reduces the balance reduces future interest.</p>
<h2>Example</h2><p>On a $26,000 loan at 6.5% for 60 months, the payment is about $509. Adding $100 a month finishes the loan in about 49 months instead of 60, and saves roughly $850 in interest.</p>
<h2>Before you pay extra</h2><ul><li><strong>Check for prepayment penalties.</strong> Some loans charge a fee for early payoff. Read your contract.</li><li><strong>Ask how extra payments are applied.</strong> Tell the lender to apply them to principal, not to the next payment.</li><li><strong>Keep your emergency fund.</strong> Do not empty your savings to pay a low-rate loan.</li></ul>
<h2>Smart ways to pay it down</h2><ul><li>Round up each payment, for example from $509 to $550.</li><li>Make one extra payment a year from a bonus or tax refund.</li><li>Pay half the payment every two weeks, if the lender allows it.</li><li>Refinance if rates have dropped or your credit has improved.</li></ul>
<h2>When it makes sense</h2><p>Paying early helps most when your rate is high. If you have a credit card balance with a higher rate, pay that first.</p>
<p>Use the calculator’s yearly schedule to see how fast your balance falls.</p>''')])
